A laboratory study found that dandelion root extract killed up to 95% of certain cancer cells within 48 hours under controlled conditions. The research was conducted in vitro (in petri dishes), meaning the results do not prove the same effect occurs in humans. Scientists emphasize that lab findings are an early step in drug discovery and do not equal a cure or treatment. Still, the results have sparked interest in how plant compounds might inspire future cancer therapies.

The bright side: nature continues to provide clues that could guide medical research — even if the real work happens much later in clinical trials. 🌼🔬

Source: Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine (University of Windsor dandelion root studies).
